TypeScript Lesson 14: Classes & Objects 🏗️
Aaj hum Classes, Objects aur Constructor seekhenge. Ye concepts Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) ka part hain aur bade applications banane mein useful hain.
1. Class kya hoti hai?
Simple example: maan lo tumhe multiple cars banani hain. Har car ka brand aur color hoga.
Har car ke liye alag code likhne ke bajaye, hum ek class ko blueprint ki tarah use kar sakte hain.





















- Class = blueprint/design
- Object = us blueprint se bani actual car
TypeScript example:
class Car {
    brand: string;
    color: string;

    constructor(brand: string, color: string) {
        this.brand = brand;
        this.color = color;
    }
}

const car1 = new Car("Tesla", "Red");
const car2 = new Car("BMW", "Black");

console.log(car1.brand);
console.log(car2.color);


Output:
Tesla
Black


2. constructor() kya hota hai?
constructor() ek special method hai jo object create karte waqt automatically execute hota hai.
class Student {
    name: string;
    age: number;

    constructor(name: string, age: number) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

const student1 = new Student("Rahul", 22);

console.log(student1.name);
console.log(student1.age);


Output:
Rahul
22


Samjho:
- new Student(...) → naya object banata hai.
- constructor(name, age) → initial values receive karta hai.
- this.name → current object ki name property.
- this.age → current object ki age property.
Memory trick: this ka matlab hai current object.
3. Class ke andar methods banana
Method ka matlab hai class ke andar function.
class Calculator {
    add(a: number, b: number): number {
        return a + b;
    }

    multiply(a: number, b: number): number {
        return a * b;
    }
}

const calculator = new Calculator();

console.log(calculator.add(10, 20));
console.log(calculator.multiply(4, 5));


Output:
30
20


Yahan add() aur multiply() dono methods hain.
4. public aur private
Ye access modifiers hain. Ye control karte hain ki class ki properties aur methods ko kahan se access kiya ja sakta hai.
class BankAccount {
    public owner: string;
    private balance: number;

    constructor(owner: string, balance: number) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public showBalance(): void {
        console.log(this.balance);
    }
}

const account = new BankAccount("Rahul", 5000);

console.log(account.owner);  // Valid
account.showBalance();       // Valid

// console.log(account.balance); // TypeScript error


Output:
Rahul
5000


- public → class ke bahar se access kar sakte ho.
- private → sirf us class ke andar access kar sakte ho.
Dhyan rahe: TypeScript ka private compile-time type checking deta hai; JavaScript runtime par strong privacy ke liye #balance jaisa private field use hota hai.
5. Shortcut: Constructor parameter properties
TypeScript mein properties declare aur assign karne ka shortcut bhi hai.
class Employee {
    constructor(
        public name: string,
        public salary: number
    ) {}
}

const emp = new Employee("Amit", 40000);

console.log(emp.name);
console.log(emp.salary);


Output:
Amit
40000


public name: string constructor ke andar likhne se TypeScript property create aur assign kar deta hai.

